    <string name="help_characters_body">These are not throttled Stockfish engines but Maia-3, a network trained on HUMAN games: it plays the moves that players of your level actually play, mistakes included. Each character adds a style — attack, trades, gambits — that COLOURS that distribution without replacing it: it never buys a blunder Maia judged unlikely.</string>
    <string name="help_characters_title">The nine characters</string>
    <string name="help_courses_body">136 courses, each split into chapters. The comments come from the positions themselves, and the percentages are those of club games. Endgames follow the same format as openings: they are positions to understand, not to memorise.</string>
    <string name="help_courses_title">Openings and endgames</string>
    <string name="help_level_body">It is Maia\'s human scale, close to Lichess\'s. It is NOT comparable to a « Stockfish level »: a Stockfish throttled to 1100 beats a Maia set to 2200, because it calculates where the other recognises. Two scales, two different things.</string>
    <string name="help_level_title">The level shown</string>
    <string name="help_offline_body">Everything. No account, no network, no data leaving the device. The engine, the puzzles, the courses and the opponents are all embedded.</string>
    <string name="help_offline_title">What stays offline</string>
    <string name="help_puzzles_body">One try by default. Three invite you to try a move « just to see », exactly the opposite of what a puzzle trains: you calculate the line to the end BEFORE you touch the piece. The three-try net is still in the settings.</string>
    <string name="help_puzzles_title">The puzzles</string>
    <string name="help_safety_body">Maia does not calculate: it can miss a mate in two or spoil an elementary endgame. Above a certain level, Stockfish takes over in FOUR cases only — a mate in one or two, an endgame with few pieces, a repetition in a winning position, and the case where the model does not answer. Below that level, missing a mate is PART of the character.</string>
    <string name="help_safety_title">The safety net</string>
    <string name="help_scanner_body">The board is found automatically by its checkerboard pattern, and you adjust the four corners if needed. Piece recognition is automatic; the SIDE TO MOVE, the CASTLING RIGHTS and EN PASSANT cannot be read from an image, and the app does not invent them — correct them yourself.</string>
    <string name="help_scanner_title">The scanner</string>
    <string name="help_training_body">Training plays a course on a single continuous board: you play YOUR side, the partner answers on the main line. Every position you play is graded on its own — a mistake means « again », a hint caps the grade at « hard » — and FSRS schedules when you see it again. The unit is the POSITION, never the line: a position learned in one opening counts in every opening it transposes to.</string>
    <string name="help_training_title">Spaced repetition</string>
    <string name="help_variants_body">They are not played by the rules of classical chess but REFEREED by an engine that knows them: the position and the legal moves come from it. That is what makes Atomic or Antichess possible without rewriting seven sets of rules.</string>
    <string name="help_variants_title">The variants</string>
